The goal is to have a small AI model manage the CPU Scheduler instead of binary management.

# Adding the AI into the Linux Kernel
We will have to create a small AI model with a Chain Of Thought so it can properly reason with itself on how to manage processes.
it has to have real reasoning, but output tokens to trigger actions. The model needs to be very light and fast, with "subagents"
# Preposed tokens
    <kill> : Kills a process, requiring approval (Pushes a notification with reasoning created by `<task> $reason &kill` beforehand.)
    <start>: Starts a process, requiring approval (Pushes a notification with reasoning created by `<task> $reason &start` beforehand.)
    <freeze>: Stops a process; essentially pauses it, but <unfreeze> can revert the process back to its unfrozen state. Approval not required, but notification is pushed.
    <task>$: Creates a subtask for another smaller model to manage. $token for tasks.
        $reason&: Task for reasoning, &token used for type.
            &kill: provides a reasoning for killing a process
            &freeze: provides a reasoning for why a process was frozen
            &start: provides a reasoning for why a process should be started.
        $shell: Allows for a shell command to be executed.
    <priority>$: Changes a task's priority
        $lower: lowers a process's priority by one level
        $raise: raises a process's priority by one level
        $max  : Makes a process take top priority
        $min  : Makes a process take lowest priority